**Definition** (Elementary Form).: A topos is a category \(\mathcal{E}\) with

1. A pullback for every diagram \(X\to B\gets Y\);
2. A terminal object \(1\);
3. An object \(\Omega\) and a monic arrow \(\operatorname{true}\colon 1\!\mapsto\!\Omega\) such that for any monic \(m\colon S\!\mapsto\!B\) there is a unique arrow \(\phi\colon B\to\Omega\) in \(\mathcal{E}\) for which the following square is a pullback: \[\begin{CD}S@>{}>{}>1\\ @V{m}V{}V@V{}V{\operatorname{true}}V\\ B@>{}>{\phi}>\Omega.\end{CD}\] (4) In this case we write \(\phi=\operatorname{char}S\) or \(\phi=\operatorname{char}m\), and call \(\phi\) the characteristic map of \(m\) (sometimes called the classifying map of \(m\)).
4. To each object \(B\) an object \(PB\) and an arrow \(\in_{B}\colon B\times PB\to\Omega\) such that for every arrow \(f\colon B\times A\to\Omega\) there is a unique arrow \(g\colon A\to PB\) for which the following diagram commutes: \[\begin{CD}A@V{B\times A}>{}>\Omega\\ @V{g^{\prime}}V{}V@V{1\times g}V{}V@V{}V{}V\\ PB@>{}>{}>B\times PB@>{}>{\in_{B}}>\Omega.\end{CD}\] (5)

